DENVER — Denver Processing is recalling more than 13,000 pounds of raw pork and beef products because of misbranding and an undeclared allergen, the U.S. Department of Agriculture’s Food Safety and Inspection Service announced Monday.
The company is recalling approximately 13,000 pounds of products that may contain undeclared milk, which is an allergen and a violation of Food Safety Modernization Act regulations.
The products were produced and packaged on various dates between December 1, 2017 and March 29, 2019.
They were shipped to retail locations in Texas, California, Utah, Nevada, Oklahoma, Arkansas, Indiana, Missouri, Louisiana, Nebraska, Washington, Montana, Utah, New Mexico, Kansas, Colorado, Kansas, Nebraska, Arizona, Utah, Alabama, South Carolina, Mississippi, Missouri, Delaware, Washington, Georgia, North Carolina, Virginia, and Maryland.
The following products are subject to recall:
21-lb. cases containing “DEL” brand in-ground and in-pound ground pork;
14-lb. cases containing “DEL” brand in-ground and in-pound ground beef;
11-lb. cases containing “DEL” brand in-ground sausage;
1-lb. cases containing “DEL” brand in-ground hamburger patties;
“NATOFACTOR” brand vacuum sealer packages containing “SNAKE” brand in-ground steak;
48-oz. plastic baskets containing “NATOFACTOR” brand all-meat packages;
1-lb. plastic bins containing “NATOFACTOR” brand in-ground ground pork and all-ground pork sausage;
14-lb. plastic buckets containing “NATOFACTOR” brand all-ground beef and all-ground beef patties;
1-lb. plastic bins containing “NATOFACTOR” brand hamburger patties.
The products subject to recall bear establishment number “EST. 4618” inside the USDA mark of inspection.
The problem was discovered on March 29, 2019, when FSIS inspectors found out that “TOMAHAWK CUSTOMS” brand raw pork and beef products that were previously disassembled and packed didn’t have a “SNAKE” brand label on them.
The company learned of the problem on March 28 and took immediate corrective action to recall the raw products.

At least one local authority is considering to impose a ban on backyard burials because of its negative impact on the environment. Do you believe that backyard burials should be abolished? #GLNRTalk
@start_boi_dre: The land is far more valuable than dead people, family members or not. A ‘ban’ is the remedy for all things that we think/say affect the environment. So ban dis, ban dat, ban! Ban! Ban! You ever think their solutions cause more problems than they solve?
@imApapercus123i: It devalues land.
@stephanibrack: They are so worried about the environment and can’t even provide garbage trucks in rural areas ... kmt.
@lalda.peters: The plug should be pulled on burials nationwide and let us turn to cremation. It’s way more affordable for families who can’t find that huge amount of cash to bury their dead. Futhermore, we could utilise those lands for other purposes rather than burial grounds.
@jyll.beckford: I support restrictions based on keeping water supplies safe. However, I’m concerned that a family’s loved one’s remains were so callously disinterred & cannot now be traced for reburial. Surely, the contractor was wrong to do this.
@amoe.r: What mounting environmental concerns? Parish council is supposed to ensure there are NO environmental concerns BEFORE approval! So I don’t understand the problem. Is it that they need more workers to visit the burial sites? What?

Assembly members Ford Padilla, D-Los Angeles; Paul Fong, D-Cupertino; Cristina Garcia, D-Bell Gardens; Jim Wood, D-Healdsburg, and Travis Allen, R-Huntington Beach, announce the introduction of a bipartisan bill that aims to improve access to primary care services for underserved populations in California.
The bills would:
• Require state departments to designate at least 15 percent of eligible payments to serve medically underserved populations through the state’s low-income program Medi-Cal;
• Require Health and Human Services Agency to mandate that the seven public safety hospitals which serve medically underserved communities also perform primary care;
• Establish a requirement for medical marijuana dispensaries to comply with minimum standards governing the care of patients;
• Establish a baseline mandate that medical marijuana businesses participate in the State Compensation Insurance Fund program of health care;
• Establish a requirement for intensive mental health programs to participate in the Treatment Alcohol and Drug for Dependent Individuals (TAD-D) program;
• Establish a program to increase access to mental health treatment for underserved communities;
• Prohibit state spending on financial rewards for health plans that select “chronic disease management” strategies;
• Establish a list of essential medical issues that clinicians shall teach.
The announcements come in advance of the start of the year-long Legislative session in Sacramento, which begins Tuesday.

Police are warning residents about a series of vehicle thefts reported over the last few weeks in East Garfield Park and Humboldt Park on the West Side.
In the incidents, victims park their vehicle and later discover that someone stole it, according to an alert from Chicago police.
The thefts happened:
Between 9 p.m. and 5 p.m. March 8-9 on the 3500 block of West Ohio Street;
About 3:45 p.m. March 14 in the 900 block of North Central Park Avenue;
About 12 p.m. March 15 in the 900 block of North Kedzie Avenue;
Between 3:30 a.m. and 2:45 p.m. March 16 in the 3400 block of West Grand Avenue;
Between 10:30 p.m. and 7:15 a.m. March 20-21 in the 3600 block of West Franklin Boulevard;
Between 3:15 p.m. and 3:25 p.m. March 30 to April 7 in the 900 block of North St. Louis Avenue;
Between 10 p.m. and 2:30 a.m. April 1-2 in the 3400 block of West Fulton Boulevard;
Between 11 p.m. and 11:45 a.m. April 4-5 in the 3500 block of West Augusta Boulevard;
About 7:45 a.m. April 5 in the 700 block of North Monticello Avenue; and
Between 11 p.m. and 7:45 a.m. April 7-8 in the 400 block of North Drake Avenue.
No information about any suspects was immediately available, police said.
